 4. 🧀 Inside Wisconsin&#x27;s big polling miss 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Wisconsin Democratic gubernatorial nominee David Crowley speaks to the press yesterday in Middleton, Wis. Photo: Kayla Wolf/AP Pollsters blew another high-stakes election: In Wisconsin, center-left David Crowley, behind 20 points in poll after poll , stunned socialist Francesca Hong in Tuesday's Democratic primary for governor, Axios' Holly Otterbein writes. 

 The reasons for the miss became clear yesterday: Surveys a week before the primary captured only a hint of a roller coaster of a race in which Crowley was gaining fast and Hong was playing defense. Two recent polls had Hong up by about 20 points over Crowley. But Crowley wound up winning by half a point (39.8% to 39.3%) in an upset that interrupted a run of primary wins by left-wing Democrats this spring.

 Former top Obama strategist David Axelrod told us: "Part of it is the old saw that we often say but rarely hear: Polling is a snapshot in time. A lot of the polls were taken too early to capture the movement." 🔎 Zoom in: The primary's final month was particularly chaotic. The party's establishment scrambled to stop Hong out of fear the state Assembly member was too far left to win in November.

 The last week of the campaign was dominated by the fallout from Hong struggling to explain to CNN why she'd once taken to social media to say "cancel Thanksgiving." 📺 Dan Pfeiffer, another former Obama adviser , noted on X and Substack that Hong — with little campaign money — didn't air TV ads during the campaign's stretch run to counter the attacks against her in the critical last few days before the primary.

 "Hong ran an inspirational grassroots campaign that mobilized people around key issues like data centers, but her failure to advertise in the final days probably cost her the election," Pfeiffer wrote. Share this story . 

 
 
