 5. 🎤 Leavitt&#x27;s White House exit 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 White House press secretary Karoline Leavitt answers questions last month. Photo: Kylie Cooper/Reuters Karoline Leavitt, the youngest White House press secretary ever, will leave at the end of the month to spend more time with her young children.

 Trump said in the surprise announcement on Truth Social that Leavitt will become one of his top outside advisers and "an influential voice within the Republican Party" heading into the midterms. Leavitt, 28, said on X that being a mother and welcoming a new baby while serving as press secretary "has been the most rewarding yet challenging season of my life, to say the least." 🔎 Behind the scenes: Multiple White House sources tell Axios' Marc Caputo that Trump hasn't picked a replacement. "He's sad to see Karoline go, and he'll hold out hope she'll decide to stay," a senior administration official said. "But he's gonna enjoy the heck out of this process of picking someone."

 Separately, longtime Trump adviser Jason Miller has been spotted in the West Wing, working as a special government employee and advising the president on policy proposals and policy messaging for midterms. 🛢️ Power moves: Brittany Kelm, one of the White House's senior energy advisers, is leaving for Sable Offshore, a small oil company with a big political footprint. Kelm is slated to head up its D.C. office as a vice president of policy and commercial programs, Marc reports. 

 
 
